# Keeps the build context small and, more importantly, keeps things out of the image that should
# never be in one. A Docker build sends the whole context to the daemon and anything copied in is
# recoverable from the published layers, so this list is a security boundary and not only a speed
# optimisation.

# Build output. Copying a host build into the image would mix Windows artifacts into a Linux
# build and silently defeat the restore-layer caching.
**/bin/
**/obj/
**/dist/
**/node_modules/

# Captured provider responses. CricketData echoes our API key back as the first field of every
# response body, so these files carry a secret whether or not anyone intended them to.
.spike/

# Run logs, for the same reason.
**/*.log

# The local archive. It is rebuilt from live data, it is large, and the container mounts its own
# at /data.
**/*.db
**/*.db-shm
**/*.db-wal

# Environment files. None of them belong in an image; configuration arrives as environment
# variables at run time.
**/.env
**/.env.*

# The frontend is deployed as static files by its own host, not from this image.
frontend/

# Nothing here is read during a build.
.git/
.github/
.vs/
.vscode/
.idea/
docs/
*.md
